          "verdict": "disputed",
          "derivation": "The Arabic word is آجُرّ ʾājurr (with variants آجُر ʾājur, آجُور ʾājūr, آجُرّة for the single brick, plural أواجير), and it is certainly a loan — the Arab lexicographers themselves treat it as muʿarrab, and the un-Arabic shape and the broken plural give it away. The disagreement is over the lender. Nourai draws it from Persian آگور; Asbaghi (PLA p.6) is the only one of his four references that agrees. Fraenkel, cited by Nourai himself and the first authority on Aramaic loans in Arabic, says the opposite in as many words: 'In Wirklichkeit aber ist آجر auch im Persischen ein aramäisches Lehnwort, und dass die Araber es von den Persern übernommen haben sollten, ist nach sonstigen Analogien nicht wahrscheinlich' — the Persian word is itself an Aramaic loan, and an Arabic borrowing from Persian is improbable; he adds that the Arabs' own claim of a Persian origin (Jawharī s.v.) is mistaken, and cites Nābigha 7,16 and Dīwān Hudhayl 66,10 for early attestation. Zimmern derives Arabic and Persian in parallel from Aramaic: 'akk. agurru gebrannter Ziegelstein : > aram. ʾāgurā (> arab. ʾāgurr, ʾāgār, desgl. pers. āgūr, armen. agur, griech. αγουρος)'. Addi Shir, whose whole book is a catalogue of Persian words in Arabic, nevertheless quotes Fraenkel p.5 for an Aramaic origin at the entry Nourai cites. Wiktionary today: Arabic آجُرّ 'from Classical Syriac ʾăḡurrā, from Akkadian agurru'. The doubled -rr of the Arabic form and the long ā both match the Aramaic emphatic-state ʾāgūrā better than Persian āgur. Nourai's own NOTE ('This word could have entered Arabic directly from Akkadian / Aramaic (AFA:5)') records the objection; the arrow as drawn is nonetheless the minority view.",
